# 258 · A run owns its agents and its share of compute: the control plane runs the loops, a light runner does the work

**Status:** `READY` · **Version:** [v0.3.0](../roadmap.md#v030) · **Depends on:** [242](_completed/242-v030-review.goal)

## What

Accepted by the owner on 2026-11-12, as the first work of v0.3.0's app. Nothing of it is built, and its design is not written yet.

Today the order is machine, then rig, then campaign, then run. A rig owns the agents, their models, the lanes and the runners, and a machine belongs to one rig. This goal asks whether that order can turn around:

1. **A run is what the owner edits.** Its workers at a time, each agent's model and its share of compute are the run's own, changed on the run, from its next trial or job.
2. **Machines are a pool.** A run asks for what it needs. A machine serves many runs through its devices: in turns at a whole device, or in parts of the machine at the same time (memory, CPUs). A device is a GPU or another kind of target, such as a microcontroller board.
3. **The agents' loops run in the control plane:** workers, planner, teachers, auditor, review panel, with the run's record and the judge's decisions. A run that builds nothing, such as research, needs no machine.
4. **A machine runs one light runner:** one program, with no container engine to set up. It does what needs the hardware: a worker's shell and files in a sandbox, builds, measurements.
5. **The control plane's store is PostgreSQL,** in place of SQLite: several services write to it at once. A run's records can be asked in plain SQL, read only.
6. **Or all on the machine:** the same control-plane parts beside the runner, for a site with no link out, or for people who want a run self-contained.

## Why

The owner, 2026-11-10:

- "If I want to change the models, lane count, machine allocation... I have to go edit the rig, when really I'm editing a run against the resources given to it."
- "I'd like to be able to run a campaign against only a portion of a gpu or resource etc... so I can run say many campaigns on a single dgx spark."
- "the core is super idle all the work is on the gpus, the agent loops require basically nothing."
- "Could runners just be a single binary no docker/podman requirements?"
- "research goal... never builds stuff... so it doesn't actually need an allocation of rig/resource it can just run agent loops."
- "IF we do this it simplifies the architecture a lot imo."

A rig's core and its runners already split this way ([A rig's core and its runners](../../_site/src/content/docs/explanation/core-and-runners.md)): the core holds the run's record, the agents and the judge's decisions, and the runners build and measure. The proposal moves the core's part into the control plane and makes the runner the only thing on a machine.

## Constraints

- **Agents never judge.** Measurement, verdicts and promotion stay in code the agents cannot write. Today separate users, the broker and the device fence keep a worker away from the judge's files and GPU on one machine ([Isolation](../../_site/src/content/docs/explanation/isolation.md)). A runner with no container must give the same wall.
- **A worker's shell is code from a model.** It runs in a sandbox, in a zone of its own, walled off from the record and from the judge's decisions. (Until 2026-11-12 this line read "in a sandbox on a runner, never inside the control plane". The owner then decided that the agents and the writing of code are in the control plane, which gets sandboxes of its own: Decisions.)
- **A restart of one service stops no run.** The control plane is services, each restarted alone (Decisions). Each picks up where it was after its own restart: a loop its trial, the scheduler its queue.
- **Keys stay in the control plane.** A machine holds none.

## Done when

- [x] The owner decides whether this goes on. 2026-11-12: yes, and before the app's screens.
- [ ] A written design answers the open questions below, with its costs and the order of its parts, and the owner agrees it. No code before that.
- [ ] Each part of the design is a goal of its own, and each is verified.
- [ ] The app's screens for Compute and a run's Setup are built on it, as the agreed mockup draws them (the [app's build plan](../app-build-plan.md)).

## Decisions

- 2026-11-10, the owner, on how a run shares a device: "This is more documentation and training than a constraint we should have in the system." The system lets a run take a whole device in turns, or a part of it beside other runs, for the judge too. The docs say what each does to a measurement, and the run's own noise figure shows it. No rule of the system forbids a choice.
- 2026-11-10, the owner: "postgres". Control's store moves from SQLite to PostgreSQL with this goal, since services that share a store need one that takes several writers. The dashboard's query of a run is plain SQL: a select and nothing else, over a small set of tables for each run (attempts, their metrics, champions, ideas, experiments, trials, notes, events), with a time limit and a row limit, through a role that reads those tables alone. Those tables are a contract. Thermite writes no query language of its own. The message bus stays the transport and the log of events ([decisions](../decisions.md#control-and-operation)).
- 2026-11-10, the owner, on restarts: "They can be separate services within the control plane... No one said monolithic is required". The control plane is services: the dashboard and the API, the agents' loops, the scheduler with the judge's decisions, the store. A restart of the dashboard or the API stops no run.
- 2026-11-10, the owner, from the whole-app mockups: the dashboard's mockups stay on this goal's model. No screen shows a rig: a run's Setup holds its agents, their models and the machines it may use, and Compute is the pool of machines. This goal is still proposed; the choice means the new screens need it, or a part of it, before they can be built as drawn.
- 2026-11-10, the owner, on the mockups' words: "instead of Machine -> GPU... probably should be machine -> device(GPU)... we may hav eother types of devices/components that need to be worked against like esp32 boards etc. we want to be able to assign devices to run experiments against vs just locked into gpus." The hierarchy is machine › device. A device has a kind: a GPU, or another kind that a target driver reaches. A run is given devices, for its judge and for its workers; nothing in the model is tied to GPUs. This is the settled decision "not every target is a GPU", carried into how compute is given to a run.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on the app's build plan, which asked whether the screens are built first on today's model with the rig behind the words, or after this goal: **"Goal 258 first."** The back end is rebuilt with no rig, and the screens are then built once, on the model they were drawn for. This goal is accepted into v0.3.0 and comes before the screens that show a run or a machine. The owner took the cost that was named with it: nothing new to test for a long time, and the old dashboard in use through all of it.
- 2026-11-12, the owner, on [the design](../goal-258-design.md), which is agreed with these answers:
  - **The screens start after the design's step 8,** when the app's model is true at the API; the last two steps go on beside them.
  - **The runner:** "for v0.3.0 keep it one runner in an image so container stay s the wall. for v0.4.0 we look at microsandbox as execution image system and bundling it into our program for distribution."
  - **The agents work in the control plane:** "claude code should still work on the control in its user zone etc and still submits code/changes to machine runners etc... only thing that runs on machines is execution that needs the machine resourcees for that. but agent loops and writing code etc can all happen in the control plane. Now control becomes a control plane... so it might need its own sandboxes and stuff..." So a worker's copy of the project, its shell and its agent program are in the control plane, in a zone of its own; a change goes to a machine as a commit; no sign-in for a model reaches a machine.
  - **Each step of the design is a goal of its own.**

## Open questions

- What gives the wall between a worker and the judge on one machine without a container: users and namespaces set up by the runner itself, or a sandbox per job. What the isolation self-test must prove there.
- Where a worker's workspace lives when its loop is in the control plane: on a runner, with each command sent there, or in a sandbox the control plane has.
- Which services the control plane has, what each needs when it serves every run (CPUs, memory, terminals for the agent programs), and what each one's restart stops.
- How a part of a GPU is given: memory and CPUs alone, or a share of its time. What the docs and the run's noise figure must say, so that the owner's choice is an informed one.
- What becomes of the rig as a word and as a thing: gone, or the name of a set of machines.
- How control's 37 migrations and its data move from SQLite to PostgreSQL, what the one-machine install gains (one more service), how backup and restore change, and what the tests use in place of an in-memory store.
- Which tables a run's query may read, with which columns, and how a change to them is announced, since saved views depend on them.
- How a deployment of today moves over, and whether both shapes run side by side for a time.
- Whether the judge's decisions may leave the machine at all for a site that wants every part of a run on its own hardware.

## Progress

- 2026-11-12: The owner agreed the design, with changes (Decisions). Its ten steps are goals 267 to 276; the runner with no container engine is goal 277, for v0.4.0. This goal is done when they are.
- 2026-11-12: [The design](../goal-258-design.md) is written, from two readings of the code: what control, the harness and the supervisor hold today; a proposal for each open question below; ten steps that each leave a working system; the five hard problems; six points for the owner. It waits for the owner's yes.
- 2026-11-12: Accepted into v0.3.0, first (Decisions). Next: the written design, for the owner's yes.
- 2026-11-10: Written from the owner's words, during the review of the dashboard's first mockups. Mockups of a run's Setup, of Compute and of the two shapes are on the design canvas the owner looks at.

